WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.000
Nicole: So, what exactly is bias?

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.340
Bias is the inclination or prejudice
for or against something or someone.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:12.379
We all have biases that often
impact our decisions, and this

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.210
also goes for cyber security.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:19.210
Abraham: Cyber security personnel have
to address what alerts to investigate,

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:24.070
what systems to patch and often these
decisions are affected by biases

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.070
which help us to react more quickly.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.290
Nicole: Imagine if the current threat
landscape shows a high level of breaches

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:34.780
by foreign hackers, then cyber security
teams might be on the lookout for these

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.559
and may miss the more common internal
or localised social engineering threat.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.790
Abraham: Threat actors use this bias to
try to exploit and threaten our security.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.830
By being aware of bias and how it
can affect our decision making, it

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.880
can help us to avoid making mistakes.

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:55.279
Nicole: Let’s have a look at some of
the different types of bias and how

00:00:55.279 --> 00:00:57.419
they may affect cyber security issues:

00:00:58.039 --> 00:01:01.690
Cognitive Bias is the result of
the brains attempt to simplify

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.880
information processing; we create
our own subjective reality due

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.499
to our perception of the input.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.820
We may see or hear something but
perceive the information differently to

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.650
someone , even though the information
we both receive is the same.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.480
Abraham: It is therefore important
to discuss things with others to see

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.450
their point of view on the situation.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.500
Availability Bias is a mental
shortcut based on past examples

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.439
relating to the available information.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.350
If we are used to hearing this
information, then we recall it quickly

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.070
and therefore it can influence our
‘go to’ solution and as such, if

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.230
a problem was in a specific area
previously, we might automatically

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.870
feel that it is the same again, rather
than looking at the bigger picture.

00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:50.500
Nicole: Availability bias is also
evident when an organisation prepares for

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.399
‘high impact’ ‘low probability events’
instead of ‘high probability’ events.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.500
High probability events should
be mostly prepared for as they

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:00.890
are more likely to happen!

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.920
Abraham: Confirmation Bias is when
more attention is paid to things that

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.489
confirm your own beliefs rather than
looking for other points of view.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.980
Nicole: Imagine investigating a
data breach by a suspected malicious

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.510
employee, then that is the evidence you
would be looking for and subsequently

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.280
you might miss the real cause.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.219
Abraham: Implicit or Unconscious Bias
can influence our bias by believing

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:27.810
in the experts especially if you
already have an association with them.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.070
If they think it’s right, then it
must be, without even challenging the

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.100
data and looking at alternative views.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.969
Nicole: Being on the side of the
majority is not always the best approach.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.409
You should look at the facts and then make
your own decisions and if they differ from

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:45.713
others identify, then look at why this is.

00:02:45.713 --> 00:02:49.090
Abraham: In cyber security
there may often be assumptions

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.850
made about users due to bias.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.689
This may involve an age approach
which could follows the adage

00:02:54.759 --> 00:02:58.619
‘older and wiser’ or conversely
older therefore ‘not up to date’.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.419
Nicole: Younger people can also be
seen as more knowledgeable of new

00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:06.810
technology, but may be reckless and
likely to get involved in risky behaviour.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.479
Both ends of the spectrum can be
right for various reasons but how

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.070
we perceive things is more often
than not, related to our bias.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.199
Abraham: Cyber security professionals
need to always investigate with

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:23.219
an open mind and not automatically
think that the user is to blame.

00:03:23.759 --> 00:03:28.320
To tackle this bias the cyber security
professional should go beyond the user and

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.349
see if the system is to blame, ultimately
making it much easier for accidents

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:35.119
to happen after all no one is perfect.

